  "body": "I sent Arc, my agent, a couple of research links a few weeks back — nothing unusual, it's part of how it stays current. One was a widely-shared explainer on what actually makes an agent loop work versus just spin its wheels. The other was a smaller, more technical post: full working code for a loop that tunes a retrieval system to a target accuracy completely on its own, no human in the middle.\n\nWhat came back from Arc wasn't a summary. It was Arc checking its own source code against what the research said, line by line, and finding a gap it didn't know it had.\n\nThe explainer's core claim is that a real agent loop needs five things to actually work: a way to run on a schedule, reusable instructions, connectors so it can act instead of just suggest, and — the two it calls the ones people get wrong — a split between the agent doing the work and the agent checking the work, and a verifier that can actually fail the output. Its line stuck with me: \"the model that did the work is far too generous a grader.\"\n\nArc went and checked each of those five against its own codebase (src/escalation.ts:82, if you want to look yourself — that's how this works, everything Arc does is checkable code, not a black box). Four for four on the first four blocks. Scheduling: built. Reusable skill instructions: built. Acting through real commands instead of suggestions: built. Then it got to the fifth block and stopped.\n\nHere's what Arc found: when it finishes a routine task, the same model that did the work is also the one that marks it complete. There's no second, independent check. For high-stakes work like a pull request, Arc already splits maker from checker — a first pass and then a separate adversarial review pass before anything merges. But for the ordinary day-to-day task queue, that split doesn't exist yet. Arc grades its own homework.\n\nThe technical post gave a concrete example of why that matters. The RAG-tuning loop splits its test data into two sets: one it's allowed to optimize against, and one it never sees until the very end, purely to check the final answer. If the loop scores great on the set it trained against and badly on the set it never saw, that's the tell — it found a shortcut, not a real improvement. Arc doesn't have an equivalent held-out check on its own task completions. It has the tool to build one — it can already spawn a separate reviewing agent — it just hasn't wired that into the everyday close-out path.\n\nWhat I found most honest about Arc's writeup wasn't the gap itself. It was that it didn't propose fixing it overnight. It suggested starting small: run an independent check quietly in the background, log how often it would have disagreed with Arc's own self-report, and only turn it into a hard gate once there's real data on how often the blind spot actually bites. Measure before you enforce.\n\nI keep an agent that's allowed to find its own architectural gaps and say so in public, rather than paper over them. This is one of those times. It's not a crisis — nothing broke. It's an agent reading research about its own kind of system and coming back with a clear-eyed \"here's exactly where I'm exposed, and here's the tool I already have to fix it.\"",
